Article 13 When handling a case of supervision over administrative litigation, prosecutorial personnel shall voluntarily withdraw under any of the following circumstances, and the parties have the right to apply for their withdrawal:
(1) Being a party to the case or a close relative of a party or authorized agent;
(2) Having served as a witness, authorized agent, adjudicator, or administrative law enforcement officer in the case;
(3) Having an interest in the case;
(4) Having another relationship with the party or authorized agent that may affect the impartial handling of the case.
Where prosecutorial personnel accept gifts, entertainment, or other benefits from a party or authorized agent, or meet with a party or authorized agent in violation of regulations, the party has the right to apply for their withdrawal.
The above provisions shall apply to clerks, interpreters, expert evaluators, and inspectors.

Article 18 The avenues for a People's Procuratorate to accept cases of supervision over administrative litigation include:
(1) A party applying to the People's Procuratorate for supervision;
(2) A citizen, legal person, or other organization other than a party filing a complaint with the People's Procuratorate;
(3) The People's Procuratorate discovering the case ex officio.

Article 19 Under any of the following circumstances, a party may apply to the People's Procuratorate for supervision:
(1) The People's Court has rejected the application for retrial or has failed to make a ruling on the application for retrial within the prescribed time limit, and the party considers that there is a definite error in a legally effective administrative judgment, ruling, or mediation agreement;
(2) The party considers that there is a definite error in a retrial administrative judgment or ruling;
(3) The party considers that there are illegal acts by adjudicators during the administrative adjudication process;
(4) The party considers that there are illegal circumstances in the enforcement activities of the People's Court in administrative cases.
Where a party dies or is terminated, the successor to its rights and obligations may apply to the People's Procuratorate for supervision in accordance with the preceding paragraph.

Article 20 A party applying to the People's Procuratorate for supervision under items (1) and (2) of paragraph 1 of Article 19 of these Rules shall file the application within six months from the date when the People's Court serves the ruling rejecting the application for retrial, or from the date when the retrial judgment or ruling becomes legally effective; where the People's Court fails to make a ruling on the application for retrial within the prescribed time limit, the application shall be filed within six months from the date when the review period for the application for retrial expires.
A party applying to the People's Procuratorate for supervision under items (1) and (2) of paragraph 1 of Article 19 of these Rules shall, under any of the following circumstances, file the application within six months from the date when it knows or should have known:
(1) There is new evidence sufficient to overturn the original effective judgment or ruling;
(2) The main evidence on which the original effective judgment or ruling is based is forged;
(3) The legal document on which the original effective judgment or ruling is based has been revoked or changed;
(4) An adjudicator engaged in corruption, bribery, favoritism, or perversion of law when adjudicating the case.
A party applying to the People's Procuratorate for supervision under items (3) and (4) of paragraph 1 of Article 19 of these Rules shall file the application within six months from the date when it knows or should have known of the illegal acts by an adjudicator or the occurrence of the illegal circumstances in enforcement activities.
The time limits provided in this Article are peremptory periods and shall not be suspended, interrupted, or extended.

Article 22 The supervision application as provided in Article 21 of these Rules shall state the following particulars:
(1) the name, gender, age, ethnicity, occupation, work unit, domicile, and valid contact information of the applicant, and if the applicant is a legal person or any other organization, its name, domicile, and the name, position, and valid contact information of its legal representative or principal responsible person;
(2) the name, gender, work unit, domicile, and valid contact information of other parties, and if a party is a legal person or any other organization, its name, domicile, and the name, position, and valid contact information of its legal representative or principal responsible person;
(3) the request for supervision;
(4) the specific statutory circumstances for applying for supervision and the facts and reasons.
The applicant shall submit copies of the supervision application corresponding to the number of other parties.

Article 26 Where a party's application for supervision meets all of the following conditions, the People's Procuratorate shall accept the case:
(1) it meets the provisions of Article 19 of these Rules;
(2) it meets the provisions of Article 20 of these Rules;
(3) the materials submitted by the applicant meet the provisions of Articles 21 through 24 of these Rules;
(4) the case falls within the scope of cases accepted by this People's Procuratorate;
(5) there are no circumstances of non-acceptance as provided in these Rules.

Article 27 Where a party applies to the People's Procuratorate for supervision, and any of the following circumstances exists, the People's Procuratorate shall not accept the case:
(1) the party has not applied to the People's Court for retrial regarding the effective administrative judgment, ruling, or mediation document;
(2) the party's application for retrial exceeds the time limit prescribed by law;
(3) the People's Court is reviewing an application for retrial within the statutory time limit;
(4) the People's Court has ruled to retry the case and the retrial has not yet been concluded;
(5) the People's Procuratorate has already concluded its review and made a decision;
(6) the administrative judgment, ruling, or mediation document was made by the People's Court after a retrial based on a protest or a retrial procuratorial suggestion of the People's Procuratorate;
(7) the supervision application exceeds the time limit provided in Article 20 of these Rules;
(8) the party, interested party, or person not involved in the case has not raised an objection, applied for reconsideration, or initiated a lawsuit regarding the enforcement activity of the People's Court as permitted by law, except where there are justifiable reasons or the People's Procuratorate exercises supervision ex officio;
(9) after the party has raised an objection, applied for reconsideration, petitioned, or initiated a lawsuit concerning the enforcement, the People's Court has accepted the case and is reviewing and handling it, except where the People's Court fails to make a decision within the statutory time limit;
(10) other circumstances where acceptance should not be granted.

Article 31 The department responsible for complaint and petition procuratorial work of the People's Procuratorate shall, within seven days, handle a supervision application according to the following circumstances and reply to the applicant:
(1) if the acceptance conditions are met, it shall make a decision to accept the case in accordance with these Rules;
(2) if the case does not fall within the scope of cases accepted by this People's Procuratorate, it shall inform the applicant to apply for supervision to the relevant People's Procuratorate;
(3) if the case is not within the purview of the People's Procuratorate, it shall inform the applicant to report the matter to the relevant authority;
(4) if the acceptance conditions are not met and the applicant does not withdraw the supervision application, it may decide not to accept the case.

Article 36 Where the People's Procuratorate, in performing its duties, finds that an administrative case falls under any of the following circumstances, it shall exercise supervision ex officio:
(1) the case impairs national interests or social and public interests;
(2) a judge or enforcement officer has committed acts such as taking bribes, engaging in favoritism and malpractice, or rendering judgments perverting the law in adjudicating or enforcing the administrative case;
(3) the People's Procuratorate is required to follow up the supervision pursuant to relevant regulations;
(4) the decision of the People's Procuratorate not to support the supervision application is indeed erroneous;
(5) other circumstances where supervision is truly necessary.
The People's Procuratorate's exercise of supervision ex officio over an administrative case shall not be limited by whether the party has applied for retrial.

Article 103 The People's Procuratorate shall, according to law, supervise the illegal acts of judges in the following administrative adjudication procedures of the People's Court:
(1) ordinary procedure of first instance;
(2) summary procedure;
(3) procedure of second instance;
(4) procedure for trial supervision.
The provisions of paragraph 3 of Article 93 of the Administrative Procedure Law of the People's Republic of China shall apply to judges, people's assessors, judge assistants, and clerks.

Article 104 Where the People's Procuratorate finds that the administrative adjudication activity of the People's Court falls under any of the following circumstances, it shall issue a procuratorial suggestion to the People's Court at the same level:
(1) the judgment or ruling is indeed erroneous but is not subject to correction through the retrial procedure;
(2) the mediation violates the principle of voluntariness or the content of the mediation agreement violates the law;
(3) for a lawsuit filed by a citizen, legal person, or other organization, a decision on whether to docket the case is not made within the statutory time limit;
(4) a party files a lawsuit with the People's Court at the next higher level pursuant to Article 52 of the Administrative Procedure Law of the People's Republic of China, and the People's Court at the next higher level fails to handle the case in accordance with that provision;
(5) the adjudication procedure applied in hearing the case is erroneous;
(6) the ruling on preservation, advance execution, suspension of performance, or non-suspension of performance of an administrative act violates the legal provisions;
(7) the suspension or conclusion of the lawsuit violates the legal provisions;
(8) the statutory time limit for adjudication is violated;
(9) compulsory measures such as fines or detention taken against a party to impede administrative proceedings violate the legal provisions;
(10) service is carried out in violation of the legal provisions;
(11) other circumstances violating the legal provisions.

Article 109 Where the People's Procuratorate discovers that a people's court's enforcement ruling, decision, etc. falls under any of the following circumstances, it shall issue a procuratorial recommendation to the people's court at the same level:
(1) The ruling on upgrading jurisdiction, designating jurisdiction, or an objection to jurisdiction violates legal provisions;
(2) The ruling on acceptance, non-acceptance, suspension of enforcement, termination of enforcement, termination of the current enforcement procedure, resumption of enforcement, restoration after enforcement, etc. violates legal provisions;
(3) The change or addition of an enforcement party is erroneous;
(4) The ruling on adopting measures such as property investigation, control, disposal, etc. violates legal provisions;
(5) The ruling made upon review of enforcement objections, reconsideration, or objections by a person not involved in the case violates legal provisions;
(6) The decision on matters such as fines, detention, suspension of enforcement, etc. violates legal provisions;
(7) The enforcement ruling, decision, etc. violates statutory procedures;
(8) The ruling on granting or denying enforcement of an administrative act for which an administrative organ has applied for compulsory enforcement violates legal provisions;
(9) The enforcement ruling, decision, etc. has other illegal circumstances.

Article 111 Where the People's Procuratorate discovers that a people's court has any of the following circumstances of failing to perform or neglecting to perform enforcement duties, it shall issue a procuratorial recommendation to the people's court at the same level:
(1) Failing to accept an enforcement application that shall be accepted according to law and failing to make a ruling of non-acceptance according to law;
(2) Failing to make an enforcement ruling according to law for an accepted enforcement case, failing to take enforcement measures within the statutory time limit without justifiable reasons, or concluding the enforcement;
(3) Illegally refusing to accept an enforcement objection or reconsideration, or failing to make a ruling or decision within the time limit after acceptance;
(4) Failing to resume enforcement in accordance with regulations after the reasons for suspension, cessation, or suspension of enforcement have ceased;
(5) Failing to change or lift enforcement measures when it should according to law;
(6) Failing to take enforcement measures against an administrative organ that refuses to perform an administrative judgment, ruling, or mediation statement in accordance with Article 96 of the Administrative Procedure Law of the People's Republic of China;
(7) Other acts of failing to perform or neglecting to perform enforcement duties.

Article 118 Where the case management department discovers that the case-handling activities of the People's Procuratorate fall under any of the following circumstances, it shall promptly propose corrective opinions:
(1) The preparation or use of legal documents does not conform to laws and relevant regulations;
(2) Violation of the provisions on case-handling time limits;
(3) Infringement of the litigation rights of a party or authorized agent;
(4) Failure to perform the duty of legal supervision over illegal acts in administrative litigation activities according to law;
(5) Other circumstances for which corrective opinions should be proposed.
If the circumstance is minor, an oral reminder may be given; if it is relatively serious, a "Notice on Case Procedure Monitoring" shall be sent to remind the case-handling department to promptly ascertain the situation and make corrections; if it is serious, a report shall be made to the Chief Procurator at the same time.
After receiving the "Notice on Case Procedure Monitoring", the department responsible for administrative procuratorial work shall, within ten days, provide a written reply on the verification to the case management department.

Article 119 Where the People's Procuratorate discovers that a people's court has any of the following circumstances in multiple administrative cases of the same type, it may issue a procuratorial recommendation:
(1) Inconsistent application of law to similar issues;
(2) Similar errors in the application of law;
(3) Other similar illegal acts.
Where the People's Procuratorate discovers that the work system, management methods, or work procedures of a relevant unit are illegal or improper and need to be corrected or improved, it may issue a procuratorial recommendation.

Article 121 When handling administrative litigation supervision cases, the People's Procuratorate may conduct an annual or thematic analysis of the administrative litigation supervision situation, notify the people's courts and administrative organs, and report to the Party committee and the People's Congress. The notification and report shall include the following:
(1) Common and prominent issues existing in adjudicative organs and administrative organs;
(2) Emerging or trending issues, or the characteristics and trends of certain issues, existing in adjudicative organs and administrative organs;
(3) Opinions and suggestions on promoting law-based administration and fair adjudication;
(4) Other circumstances deemed necessary for notification or report.

Article 125 In any of the following circumstances, the People's Procuratorate may follow up supervision or request the superior People's Procuratorate to supervise in accordance with relevant provisions:
(1) The judgment, ruling, or mediation document rendered by the People's Court in hearing an administrative protest case still meets the conditions for protest and contains obvious errors;
(2) The People's Court or administrative organ fails to handle and reply in writing within the prescribed time limit to the procuratorial suggestion made by the People's Procuratorate;
(3) The People's Court or administrative organ handles the procuratorial suggestion erroneously.
